ARTICLES = [
{"slug":"netherlands-ksa-google-eu-licensed-operator-whitelist-2026",
"title":"KSA and Google Plan an EU Gambling Whitelist",
"category":"Regulatory",
"excerpt":"The Dutch KSA is working with Google on an EU-wide list of licensed operators to identify and block unlicensed gambling advertisers.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-20T06:10:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["netherlands","germany"],
"related_firms":["kalff-katz-and-franssen","stibbe","akd-benelux-lawyers"],
"body":[
"In commentary dated 14 October 2026, the chairman of the Dutch Gambling Authority (KSA), Michel Groothuizen, set out a plan to build an EU-wide list of licensed gambling operators together with Google. The purpose is to let the platform identify and block advertisers that hold no licence. The idea grew out of meetings in Dublin earlier this year, at which European regulators, Google and Meta agreed to form a joint task force. No launch date has been announced, and the mechanics of how the list would be maintained or consumed by Google's advertising systems have not been published.",
"The proposal is significant because it shifts the centre of gravity of enforcement from the operator to the intermediary. The KSA has openly acknowledged that it struggles to collect fines from unlicensed operators established outside the Netherlands. According to the regulator's own figures, about 90 per cent of players now use only licensed sites five years after the Dutch online market opened, yet roughly half of online gambling spending still goes to illegal operators. A whitelist addresses the problem at the point of visibility rather than at the point of collection.",
"Mr Groothuizen was candid about the commercial reality. He argued that platforms profit from illegal gambling advertising and have little incentive to restrict it, and that voluntarily giving it up would run directly against their business model. He pointed to Dutch law and the EU Digital Services Act as possible bases for action, while accepting that enforcement against the very largest platforms rests with the European Commission and that litigation could take as long as five years. A cooperative list is therefore presented as the faster route, with formal proceedings held in reserve.",
"For licensed operators the practical consequence is that licence status becomes a data point that platforms can verify automatically. Google already requires gambling advertisers to be certified for each targeted country, and a cross-border register would extend that logic. Operators should expect to be asked to confirm their licence details, their brand and domain portfolio and any affiliate or white-label arrangements, and should audit now whether every domain and trading name used in paid media is correctly recorded with the regulator that issued the licence. Mismatches between a licensed entity and the brand shown in an advertisement are the most likely cause of wrongful blocking.",
"The risk is equally acute for affiliates and media buyers. If an operator is not on the list, advertising for it may simply stop working across the EU, regardless of whether the operator is lawfully licensed in a jurisdiction that the list does not recognise. The treatment of licences issued outside the EU, such as those from Curaçao or the UK, is the obvious open question, as is whether a national licence in a closed or partly open market such as Germany or Finland will be treated as sufficient for advertising directed at other member states. Operators in those positions should seek early engagement through their counsel and trade bodies.",
"There are also fairness and process questions that a list-based system must answer. A decision by a platform to refuse advertising because of a perceived absence from a register has real commercial effect, yet it is not an administrative decision subject to appeal. Counsel should expect operators to ask for a clear route to correct errors, and regulators to be pressed on who is accountable when a licensed business is wrongly excluded. Data protection and competition considerations will also arise if a single platform becomes the practical gatekeeper of market access.",
"The direction of travel is nonetheless clear. Regulators are converging on the view that advertising channels, payment rails and app stores are the most effective control points over unlicensed supply. Licensed operators should treat verified licence status as a core compliance asset, map every advertising destination to a licensed entity, and review affiliate agreements so that they can require partners to comply promptly with any platform-level verification process that emerges from the KSA initiative."
]},
{"slug":"google-prediction-market-ad-ban-connecticut-fifth-state-2026",
"title":"Google Bans Prediction Market Ads in Connecticut",
"category":"Compliance",
"excerpt":"Google has made Connecticut the fifth US state excluded from its prediction market ads policy, adding a platform layer to state action.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-20T07:25:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["united-states"],
"related_firms":["greenberg-traurig-llp","fox-rothschild-llp","covington-and-burling-llp"],
"body":[
"A Google policy notice states that advertising for prediction market contracts and related products is prohibited in Connecticut with effect from 7 October 2026. Connecticut becomes the fifth US state to be excluded from the programme under which Google has accepted prediction market advertising from federally regulated companies since 21 January 2026. The notice gives no reason for the decision and does not define what counts as a related product, which leaves advertisers to interpret its scope for themselves.",
"The sequence of exclusions is instructive. Nevada was removed by 10 February without a separate announcement, Ohio followed on 2 June, Michigan and New York on 13 July, and Connecticut on 7 October. Each of those states has taken some form of regulatory or enforcement position against sports event contracts offered outside the state licensing system. In Connecticut's case, the Department of Consumer Protection had told nine platforms about a month earlier to stop advertising, offering or promoting sports event contracts. Google has not said that it acted because of that instruction, but the timing invites the inference that state regulatory pressure is now being reflected in platform policy.",
"For the platforms concerned, the legal question of whether sports event contracts are swaps regulated exclusively by the CFTC or gambling subject to state law remains unresolved and is being litigated across several federal circuits. A platform advertising policy sidesteps that debate. Google's eligibility test requires an advertiser to be a CFTC-designated contract market or an NFA-authorised brokerage and to hold Google certification for each targeted location. Federal status is therefore necessary but no longer sufficient, because the state in which the user is located now decides whether an advertisement may run at all.",
"Operators and their agencies should draw several compliance lessons. Geo-targeting needs to be exact and auditable, because an advertisement served to a Connecticut user after 7 October is a policy breach that can put an account at risk. Campaign structures should be reviewed state by state, with a documented process for responding to policy updates that are published without notice, as the Nevada removal was. Affiliate and influencer partners must be told in writing which states are excluded, and contractual indemnities should cover losses caused by non-compliant placements.",
"There is also a regulatory spillover risk for licensed sportsbooks that offer or plan to offer prediction products alongside their regulated betting. State regulators look at the whole footprint of a licensee, and marketing of an unlicensed product in a state that has objected to it may be cited in suitability reviews. Operators should separate brand assets, audiences and consent flows between licensed sports wagering and event-contract products, and should keep records showing that the two are not cross-promoted in restricted states.",
"The episode also shows how enforcement is migrating towards intermediaries. State regulators with limited reach over federally registered exchanges can still influence the channels through which those exchanges acquire customers, whether through cease-and-desist letters, advisory notices to payment providers or complaints to platforms. We expect further exclusions as more states act, and counsel should assume that a single regulator letter can now have a national marketing consequence within weeks.",
"The practical recommendation is to treat platform policy as a primary source of regulatory risk alongside statute and case law. Maintain a live matrix of state positions and platform eligibility, assign ownership for monitoring policy changes, and be prepared to move spend quickly. Where a prediction product is central to the business, contingency planning for loss of paid search and social channels in additional states should form part of the 2027 budget and compliance plan."
]},
{"slug":"finland-wildz-first-licence-clearance-claim-2027-market-opening",
"title":"Wildz Claims First Finnish Licence Clearance",
"category":"Licensing",
"excerpt":"Wildz says it has been cleared for a Finnish licence, but the regulator has not confirmed. What applicants should read into the claim.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-20T08:40:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["finland"],
"related_firms":["bird-and-bird","cms","taylor-wessing"],
"body":[
"On 16 October 2026 Wildz Group announced that it is the first operator cleared for a Finnish gambling licence, relying on a formal letter dated 15 October 2026. The statement is a company disclosure. As at the time of writing the National Police Board, which acts as licensing authority until the end of June 2027, has not published any decisions or independently confirmed the claim. Applicants and their advisers should therefore treat the announcement as an indication of process rather than a verified milestone.",
"The wider timetable is well defined. Parliament approved the new Gambling Act on 16 December 2025 and the President signed it on 16 January 2026. The National Police Board began accepting applications on 1 March 2026 and had received 75 by September 2026, up from 50 in June. Each applicant pays a non-refundable processing fee of EUR 29,000. The licensed market opens to private operators on 1 July 2027, and approved operators may offer online casino and betting services to Finnish players for five years from that date. Veikkaus retains exclusivity over lotteries, scratch cards and land-based casino gaming.",
"What a pre-opening clearance letter actually means is the central legal question. The grant of a licence that takes effect only on 1 July 2027 differs from permission to trade today, and nothing in the new framework relaxes the existing prohibition on marketing gambling to Finnish consumers. The Police Board has issued conditional fines of up to EUR 1.2 million against companies and EUR 200,000 against individuals for the promotion of unlicensed gambling this year. An operator that publicises a licence outcome in a way that reads as an invitation to Finnish players before the opening date risks being treated as in breach of the current Lottery Act.",
"Applicants should therefore review all investor, press and affiliate communications carefully. Statements about licensing progress are legitimate and often required by listing rules, but they should be addressed to the investor or trade audience, kept factual, and avoid language that could be read as promoting gambling services in Finland. Websites targeting Finnish users, affiliate content and social media should remain compliant with the current regime until the licence takes effect, and counsel should sign off any announcement that names Finland.",
"The handover of licensing functions from the Police Board to the Finnish Supervisory Agency at the end of June 2027 creates a second area of practical risk. Conditions imposed on a licence, reporting obligations and the supervisory relationship will all be transferred, and operators should expect a different institutional style from the new authority. Compliance programmes should be built to the statute and published guidance instead of the preferences of the first reviewer, and applicants should document every exchange with the Board so that commitments can be evidenced to the successor.",
"Competitively, early clearance matters because the first licensed entrants will have the longest period to prepare integrations with payment providers, identification services and the player-protection tooling that Finnish law will require. It does not, however, give any priority in the market, since all licensed operators will be able to start on the same day. Operators that have applied should use the months before opening to complete their systems, finalise responsible gambling controls and prepare for the marketing restrictions that will accompany the licences.",
"Our advice to the sector is to treat the 75 applications as the start of an extended supervisory relationship. Obtain written confirmation of any outcome, understand exactly which entity, brand and domains are covered, diarise the 1 July 2027 commencement and plan communications so that no commercial activity directed at Finnish consumers begins before that date."
]},
{"slug":"malta-mga-hacksaw-type-3-approval-prediction-markets-framework-gap",
"title":"MGA Type 3 Approval Opens B2B Prediction Markets",
"category":"Licensing",
"excerpt":"Hacksaw's Type 3 approval puts peer-to-peer prediction markets inside Malta's licence framework, though no dedicated rules exist yet.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-20T09:15:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["malta","gibraltar"],
"related_firms":["camilleri-preziosi","wh-partners","ellul-and-co","hassans-international-law-firm"],
"body":[
"On 15 October 2026 Hacksaw announced a B2B peer-to-peer prediction markets platform following an extension of its Malta Gaming Authority approval. The group's Maltese subsidiary, HGMT Limited, already held a B2B critical gaming supply licence covering Type 1 casino games, and the MGA's public register now also lists Type 3, which covers pool betting including betting exchange. The platform allows operators to offer prediction markets under their own brands while Hacksaw supplies the technology, infrastructure and a pooled volume framework.",
"The legal structure is worth noting. The register contains no separate prediction markets category, so the approval falls under existing Type 3 rules. In substance, Malta has permitted a supplier to provide peer-to-peer event-outcome trading through a licence class designed for pool and exchange betting. That is a pragmatic reading of the existing law, but it also means that the product is regulated by analogy rather than by purpose-built requirements covering contract design, settlement, market integrity or the treatment of customer funds.",
"The regulatory context is moving quickly. In June 2026 regulators from Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, the Netherlands, Poland, Portugal, Spain and Switzerland signed a joint declaration to act together against unlicensed prediction platforms. Malta was not a signatory. Gibraltar introduced a dedicated prediction markets framework on 13 July 2026, while Malta has said that it is exploring its own approach but, as at 16 October 2026, had published neither a draft law nor a consultation. Operators looking to use Maltese infrastructure therefore face a licensing position that is clear for suppliers but uncertain for downstream use.",
"The critical point for operators is that a B2B supplier's approval does not authorise the business-to-consumer offer in any particular market. A B2C operator wishing to launch a prediction product must still hold the permissions needed in each jurisdiction where customers are located, and many of those jurisdictions, including those party to the June declaration, have signalled that they regard unlicensed prediction products as illegal gambling. Using a Maltese-approved platform will not shield an operator from enforcement in a market that has taken that view.",
"Operators should accordingly conduct jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction analysis before launch. Key questions include whether the contract is a bet, a financial instrument or both under local law, whether the operator's existing licence covers peer-to-peer or exchange betting, what product approvals and technical certifications are required, and how local advertising rules apply. In several markets, a licence for fixed-odds betting does not extend to exchange-style trading between customers, so an operator may need an additional authorisation or may be unable to proceed at all.",
"Contract design and supplier due diligence also require attention. Operators should review how the supplier's volume framework pools liquidity across brands, who bears market-making and settlement risk, how customer money is segregated, and what integrity monitoring covers event definitions and resolution sources. Disputes over how an event is resolved are a known source of complaints in prediction products, and the commercial agreement should allocate responsibility for resolution decisions and for regulatory enquiries clearly.",
"The commercial signal is that Malta remains willing to accommodate new product models within existing licence architecture, as it did with crypto and live dealer content in the past. The legal signal is a caution: product approval without a dedicated framework tends to be followed by prescriptive rules once volumes grow. Suppliers and operators should engage with the MGA early, keep a record of the basis on which products were approved and build their compliance programmes to anticipate the stricter integrity and consumer-protection standards that a formal framework is likely to bring."
]},
{"slug":"matchbook-betdaq-acquisition-exchange-closure-customer-funds-2026",
"title":"Betdaq Exchange Closes: Customer Funds Checklist",
"category":"Market Entry",
"excerpt":"Matchbook's operator is acquiring the Betdaq brand as the exchange shuts on 21 October. What the closure means for funds and compliance.",
"publish_date":"2026-10-20T10:05:00Z",
"related_jurisdictions":["united-kingdom","ireland"],
"related_firms":["harris-hagan","wiggin-llp","pinsent-masons-llp"],
"body":[
"On 14 October 2026 it was announced that Triplebet, the operator of Matchbook, is acquiring the Betdaq brand, which was founded by Dermot Desmond in 2000. Financial terms were not disclosed. The Betdaq exchange stops trading on 21 October 2026, and customers have until 4 November 2026 to withdraw their balances. Betdaq accounts will not migrate to Matchbook, so customers who wish to move must open a new account. The published information does not describe regulatory filings or name the jurisdictions concerned.",
"The transaction is a useful case study in how betting exchange consolidation works in practice. An acquisition of a brand and customer relationships, as distinct from a purchase of the licensed company, does not usually involve a change of control approval for the buyer. It does, however, trigger regulatory obligations for the seller, which must wind down a regulated activity in an orderly way. Under licence conditions in most mature markets, an operator that ceases to offer services is expected to notify the regulator, communicate clearly with customers and return funds promptly.",
"The timetable raises several practical points. A two-week window between the end of trading and the withdrawal deadline is short for customers with open positions, pending settlements, dormant accounts or limited access to their email. Regulators in the United Kingdom and elsewhere expect closure communications to be clear and repeated, to explain how balances can be withdrawn, and to say what will happen to money not claimed by the deadline. Operators should plan for tracing efforts, alternative payment routes where a card or bank account has expired, and a documented policy for unclaimed balances.",
"Customer fund protection is the central legal issue. Under UK licence conditions, customer funds must be held in accordance with the disclosed level of protection, and a closure does not suspend that duty. Any balance not withdrawn by 4 November remains the customer's money and should not be treated as revenue. Counsel should confirm where and how balances are held, who is responsible for them after the deadline, and whether any residual balances will be transferred, retained in a segregated account or dealt with under a statutory or regulator-approved scheme.",
"Data protection and marketing compliance are the second area. Because accounts will not transfer, the buyer cannot treat the Betdaq customer base as its own. Any contact with former customers about the new service needs a valid lawful basis, and transferring personal data between group companies or to the acquirer requires a documented basis and appropriate notices. Marketing to customers who had self-excluded or whose accounts were restricted for safer gambling reasons must be avoided entirely, and the acquirer should confirm how such flags are handled.",
"For the buyer, the acquisition illustrates a lower-risk route to growing exchange liquidity, since it avoids integrating licences, platforms and legacy liabilities. The trade-off is that customer retention is uncertain when accounts do not migrate, and brand assets alone do not bring regulatory permissions. The buyer should check any restrictions attached to the brand, domain and associated intellectual property, confirm that its own licence permits the products it intends to offer to the incoming customers, and ensure that onboarding applies full customer due diligence rather than relying on the seller's records.",
"Our recommendation for operators contemplating a similar wind-down or brand sale is to engage with the regulator before announcing, to build the customer communications plan around the longest realistic withdrawal period, to ring-fence funds and to document the allocation of liabilities in the sale agreement. Buyers should verify what they are actually acquiring and ensure that any outreach to the former customer base is lawful, proportionate and consistent with safer gambling duties."
]},
]
